          "text": "# Why CUDA Graph Replay Can Be Faster Than Eager Launch\n\nWhen you launch kernels eagerly, each `cudaLaunchKernel` call goes through the CUDA runtime and driver, which must validate arguments, set up the launch configuration, and submit the work to the GPU's hardware queue. On the CPU side this costs on the order of a few microseconds per launch, and on the GPU side each kernel is scheduled independently, with the GPU front-end processing each grid launch as a separate command. For a workload made of many small kernels\u2014say hundreds of short elementwise ops, reductions, or a deep inference graph\u2014the CPU launch overhead and the GPU-side scheduling gaps can easily dominate the actual compute time. The GPU may also sit idle between kernels while waiting for the next command to arrive, especially if the CPU can't keep up.\n\nA CUDA graph captures a sequence of operations (kernel launches, memcpys, events, and their dependencies) into a single DAG, then \"instantiates\" it into an executable form. Replaying the graph submits the whole DAG to the GPU with one API call. This eliminates most per-launch CPU overhead, because the driver no longer has to re-validate and re-schedule each node individually\u2014",
